--TEST--
`Core\Hash::hmac` takes spec § 11's `StrongDigest`, so a legacy `Core\Hash::Digest` case does not compile and the diagnostic names every case that does — spec § 11, `rule:types/enum-case-type`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `StrongDigest` is not a second enum — it is the closed subset of `Digest`,
// stated as `rule:types/enum-case-type`'s union of enum-case types. So `Digest::Sha256` is
// one value that both `of` and `hmac` take, and `Digest::Md5` is refused
// here rather than at run time, with the accepted set generated from the
// parameter's own type.
bytes $key = Core\Encoding::fromHex("4a656665");
bytes $mac = Core\Hash::hmac("message", $key, Core\Hash::Digest::Md5);
echo Core\Encoding::toHex($mac), "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `Core\Hash::Digest::Sha224|Core\Hash::Digest::Sha256|Core\Hash::Digest::Sha384|Core\Hash::Digest::Sha512|Core\Hash::Digest::Sha512_224|Core\Hash::Digest::Sha512_256|Core\Hash::Digest::Sha3_224|Core\Hash::Digest::Sha3_256|Core\Hash::Digest::Sha3_384|Core\Hash::Digest::Sha3_512`, found `Core\Hash::Digest`
%A
